{"author":{"id":"dec876858d28babbadcead4137861bf586626b8518cbce9c5f4fc52ac3b424b8"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1794137235,"nonce":"1EWHsI+P1BgZThdBu8gBV22Mu+Q=","title":"no_std audit: what Blinc's core crates need to build without std","message":"Requested by users targeting embedded devices. Rough std footprint today (source greps, not a build): blinc_core 10 of 20 files use std sync, time or io and have 118 Mutex, RwLock, OnceLock, LazyLock or thread_local mentions; blinc_layout 87 of 178 files and 313 mentions; blinc_animation 4 of 11 and 23; blinc_theme 5 of 33 and 39; blinc_css 4 of 70 and 4; blinc_text 7 of 15 and 10; blinc_paint none.\n\nNeeded: decide the target (core, alloc, no threads?) and which crates are in scope; replace std locks with critical-section or spin locks behind a small abstraction and std collections with alloc or hashbrown; remove global statics that assume threads; check that taffy, nom, slotmap, smallvec and the font crates build without std (harfrust and skrifa look better for this than rustybuzz, ttf-parser and swash; unverified); gate everything else behind a std feature. Start with blinc_core, blinc_animation, blinc_css and blinc_paint, then layout.\n\nRelated: the CPU raster backend issue and the lean GPU stack plan.","files":null}]}